Introduction {#intro}
=====================

Websites have been installable on various platforms for well over a decade.
When installed, a website is added to the home screen, taskbar, dock, or launcher of the operating system, and registered with it.
When launched, it can appear as a standalone application: in its own window, full-screen, or whichever presentation is customary on the operating system.

From the user's perspective, the process of installing a website works very differently depending on the browser used, and might not be obvious at all.
Website authors report that they resort to native apps instead, and regularly ask for programmatic APIs that let them trigger install prompts themselves.

This finding is intended to guide user agent and operating system vendors.

NOTE: This finding focuses on same-origin installs.
Cross-origin installs are currently not widely implemented across the platform.
This topic might be revisited in a future version of this finding.


Installation is a core capability of browsers {#core-part-of-browsers}
======================================================================

Installable websites date back to at least 2008 (see [[INSTALLABLE-WEBAPPS#introduction]]).
By now, the installation of websites is possible on all relevant engines and operating systems.
The [[APPMANIFEST inline]], developed since 2013, allows website authors to specify metadata for installable web applications.

Installed websites fulfill a user need that otherwise can only be addressed by native apps: a persistent presence on the device, and launching from the same place as other applications.
Unlike native apps, they do so while staying within the web's security and privacy model, including its permission model and sandbox.

Given well over a decade of implementation experience and standardization, we declare the installation of websites a core capability of general-purpose browsers, and we strongly encourage them to offer it.

NOTE: We do not have a position on installation from specialized user agents, such as text-mode browsers, kiosks, or web views embedded in applications, although they can still choose to offer installation.


All websites should be installable {#all-websites-should-be-installable}
========================================================================

The TAG believes that all websites should be easily installable, in an interoperable way across all browsers.

The [[APPMANIFEST inline]] currently only defines installation for websites that provide one.
Installation of websites without a manifest is possible in all major browsers, but is not fully specified, so the result might differ between browsers.
We encourage the web community to specify this case.


Recommendations for user agent and operating system providers {#recommendations-for-providers}
==============================================================================================

In this section, we set out our recommendations for user agent and operating system vendors.

## Obvious Install UI ## {#obvious-install-ui}

We encourage [[!user-agents inline]] to make installation an obvious affordance to users.
Specifically, we encourage the install option to clearly name the action, and to be easily reachable from the browser's UI.
An entry inside a menu with an unrelated label (such as <samp>Share</samp>), or several menu layers deep, is not sufficient.
We also encourage user agents to inform their users about what happens when they decide to install a website.
The exact UI is left to user agents.

A stable and obvious affordance also benefits website authors.
They have repeatedly reported that updating their install instructions after a new browser or operating system release is tedious.

## Engine Fairness ## {#engine-fairness}

We encourage operating system providers to give all user agents the same opportunity to install websites to the home screen, taskbar, dock, or launcher.
Specifically, we encourage third-party browsers to have access to the same install mechanisms as the platform's default browser.

Additionally, we encourage operating system providers to launch installed websites using the same browser that initiated the installation.
For example, if the user asks their browser to keep them logged in across the installation operation, that's only possible if the installed site launches in the original browser.
The user had their own reasons to choose a particular user agent to perform the installation, and we encourage that choice to be respected across launches.

## No Capability Gating ## {#no-capability-gating}

Nearly all web features that are available to installed websites should also be usable by websites that haven't been installed.

The exceptions are features that
1. control part of the launcher surface to which the website is installed or
2. add an option somewhere else in the operating system.

Even the second should only motivate exceptions when there's no other clear way for users to manage that option beyond uninstalling the website.

<aside class="example" id="badging-api-example">
The Badging API (see [[BADGING]]) is exceptional because it adds a badge to the application's icon and therefore requires an icon to be present (first exception).
</aside>

<aside class="example" id="push-api-example">
The Push API (see [[PUSH-API]]) is exceptional because some operating systems might register applications allowed to send push notifications in a central surface (second exception).
However, it might be just as easy to revoke the permission for push notifications from that surface as to uninstall the website.
</aside>

The role of programmatic install APIs {#programmatic-install-apis}
==================================================================

Users often do not know that their browser can install websites, and browsers often do not know the appropriate time to inform them (see [[CHROME-INSTALL-TELEMETRY]]).
Websites have more information about whether visitors are likely to want to install the site and when it might be an optimum time to educate those visitors (see [[CHROME-ANDROID-INSTALL-FUNNEL]]).
However, websites also have incentives to nag users.

Website authors repeatedly ask for an API that lets them trigger the browser's install flow themselves.
Chromium-based browsers have exposed such an API since 2015, the [`beforeinstallprompt`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/beforeinstallprompt_event) event (see [[MANIFEST-INCUBATIONS]]).
Its design stems from a time when browsers prompted users spontaneously, and when installation was subject to meeting certain criteria, such as the presence of a service worker.
Since then, browsers have found that users do not want these spontaneous prompts, and only show them after the website has asked for them.
The Web Applications Working Group has consensus that installation should not depend on install criteria. That is, any website is installable.
What remains is a browser-sent event that provides a handle to trigger the install prompt, which is a rather unusual design.

The TAG has reviewed several proposals for new programmatic install APIs, both [same-origin](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/888) and [cross-origin](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/946), most recently the [manifest-first Web Install API](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1245).
We [encourage vendors](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/888#issuecomment-2316801231) to explore same-origin programmatic install APIs, as long as they trigger the user agent's own install flow, with the user agent retaining control over timing, eligibility, and abuse mitigation.
We still encourage a user agent to follow the recommendations in [[#obvious-install-ui]] even if it provides such an API.
